\description{
Draws a publication-quality ordination diagram in the style of CANOCO
(ter Braak and Smilauer 2012): a biplot of sites and species for
unconstrained methods and a triplot with environmental arrows for
constrained ones, with equal axis scaling, zero lines and the
variation explained by each axis in the axis titles. Species are drawn
as arrows for linear methods (PCA, RDA, PCoA, db-RDA), whose scores are
directions of increase, and as points for unimodal methods (CA, CCA,
DCA) and NMDS, whose scores are optima.
}
\details{
Groups of sites can be summarised with ellipses, convex hulls or
spiders. Ellipses follow \code{\link[vegan:ordiellipse]{vegan::ordiellipse()}}: \code{"sd"} covers one
standard deviation of the points, \code{"ci"} the region expected to hold
\code{level} of the points, and \code{"se"} a \code{level} confidence region for the
group centroid.

How to read the lines:
\itemize{
\item red arrows from the origin: species (linear methods only);
\item dark arrows from the origin: quantitative environmental variables;
\item dark triangles: centroids of factor levels (for example years);
\item coloured outlines: ellipses or convex hulls around groups of sites,
and, with \code{spider = TRUE}, lines from each group centroid to its sites;
\item faint dotted grey lines: label leaders, joining a label that had to
be moved to avoid overlap to its point. They carry no information.
}

Every option is an argument, and the result is an ordinary ggplot that
can be changed further with \code{+}.
}
